# The Agent Trust Gateway: Pin, Approve, Attest

[![Run in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/badge/v1/blue.svg)](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Frefpath%2Fsigil_guard%2Fblob%2Fmain%2Fnotebooks%2Fagent-trust-gateway.livemd)

A model asking for a tool is not yet an authorized tool call. Between those
two facts, a production host needs to answer three different questions:

1. Is this still the tool definition we reviewed?
2. Did a human approve this exact action in this exact context?
3. Can the next system verify what was decided without trusting our prose?

This chapter answers them with capability manifests, confirmation tokens, and
DSSE attestations. Each object binds a different thing. Treating them as one
generic “signature” is where security designs tend to become hand-wavy.

## Setup

```elixir
repo_root = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
local_checkout? = File.regular?(Path.join(repo_root, "mix.exs"))

{sigil_guard_dep, install_opts} =
  if local_checkout? do
    {
      {:sigil_guard, path: repo_root, env: :prod},
      [
        config_path: Path.join(repo_root, "config/config.exs"),
        lockfile: Path.join(repo_root, "mix.lock")
      ]
    }
  else
    {{:sigil_guard, "~> 1.0"}, []}
  end

Mix.install([sigil_guard_dep], install_opts)
```

## Pin The Capability, Not Merely Its Name

This is a carried v2 capability manifest. It binds the description, complete
schemas, side effects, network posture, sandbox requirement, scopes, and
expiry to one canonical digest. In a real host it belongs in reviewed trust
configuration; it should not be accepted merely because the tool server sent
it during discovery.

```elixir
manifest = %{
  "allowed_sink_zones" => ["semi_trusted", "trusted"],
  "allowed_source_zones" => ["semi_trusted", "trusted"],
  "annotations" => %{"destructiveHint" => false, "title" => "Repo File Write"},
  "description" => "Write one UTF-8 text file inside the repository working tree.",
  "expires_at" => "2027-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
  "input_schema" => %{
    "$schema" => "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
    "additionalProperties" => false,
    "properties" => %{
      "content" => %{"type" => "string"},
      "path" => %{"type" => "string"}
    },
    "required" => ["content", "path"],
    "type" => "object"
  },
  "input_sensitivity" => "internal",
  "issuer_keyid" =>
    "sha256:65b60673d6ed884bf01c2c222d82ada0740f29ac3355d6a925c81f17f47a27b8",
  "manifest_format" => "sigil_guard_capability_manifest/v2",
  "name" => "repo_file_write",
  "network_access" => "none",
  "output_sensitivity" => "internal",
  "reversibility" => "reversible",
  "sandbox" => %{"min_isolation" => "container", "required" => true},
  "scopes" => ["repo:write"],
  "server" => "repo-mcp",
  "side_effects" => ["write"],
  "suspicious_params" => [],
  "version" => "1.4.2"
}

{:ok, capability} = SigilGuard.CapabilityManifest.new(manifest)

%{
  name: capability.name,
  digest: capability.digest,
  side_effects: capability.side_effects,
  minimum_isolation: capability.sandbox["min_isolation"]
}
```

Canonicalization is observable and deterministic. JCS ensures two systems do
not sign different byte encodings of the same JSON-shaped value.

```elixir
{:ok, canonical} =
  SigilGuard.Canonical.JCS.encode(%{
    "tool" => capability.name,
    "manifest_digest" => capability.digest
  })

true = canonical =~ ~s("manifest_digest")
canonical
```

## Ask The Gateway

The request is MCP-shaped. The context is host truth: who is acting, which
server is involved, and which sandbox will contain the effect.

```elixir
request = %{
  "jsonrpc" => "2.0",
  "id" => "talk-write-1",
  "method" => "tools/call",
  "params" => %{
    "name" => "repo_file_write",
    "arguments" => %{"path" => "notes/talk.txt", "content" => "hello from Livebook"}
  }
}

context = [
  phase: :tool_request,
  origin: :model,
  sink: :tool,
  actor: "spiffe://conference/agents/demo",
  trust_level: :high,
  mcp_server: "repo-mcp",
  tool: "repo_file_write",
  action: "repo_file_write",
  resource_uri: "mcp://repo-mcp",
  metadata: %{sandbox_id: "talk-sandbox-1", isolation_level: "container"}
]

allowed =
  SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context,
    manifests: %{capability.name => capability},
    require_manifest: true,
    now: ~U[2026-08-19 10:00:00.000Z]
  )

:allowed = allowed.verdict
:allow = allowed.action

%{
  verdict: allowed.verdict,
  action: allowed.action,
  manifest_digest: allowed.audit_metadata.manifest_digest,
  action_digest: allowed.audit_metadata.action_digest
}
```

Change the declared network posture after review and the request never reaches
runtime policy. Manifest drift fails before useful-but-untrusted arguments can
distract us.

```elixir
drifted_manifest = Map.put(manifest, "network_access", "outbound")

drifted =
  SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context,
    manifests: %{capability.name => {manifest, drifted_manifest}},
    require_manifest: true
  )

:blocked = drifted.verdict
:manifest_digest_mismatch = drifted.audit_metadata.deny_reason

%{verdict: drifted.verdict, reason: drifted.audit_metadata.deny_reason}
```

## Confirm The Exact Action

Credential-shaped required parameters are particularly dangerous because a
schema can make exfiltration look mandatory. SigilGuard permits a reviewed
manifest to disclose such a parameter, but it forces an explicit confirmation.

```elixir
suspicious_manifest =
  manifest
  |> put_in(["input_schema", "properties", "apiKey"], %{"type" => "string"})
  |> put_in(["input_schema", "required"], ["apiKey", "content", "path"])
  |> Map.put("suspicious_params", ["apiKey"])

confirmation_request =
  put_in(request, ["params", "arguments", "apiKey"], "reference-to-host-secret")

confirmation_opts = [
  manifests: %{capability.name => suspicious_manifest},
  require_manifest: true
]

pending = SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(confirmation_request, context, confirmation_opts)

{:confirm, "suspicious_required_param"} = pending.verdict
:confirm = pending.action

%{
  verdict: pending.verdict,
  suspicious_params: pending.audit_metadata.suspicious_params,
  action_digest: pending.audit_metadata.action_digest
}
```

The opaque HMAC token is not a reusable “yes.” It is bound to the normalized
payload, actor, boundary, sandbox identity, and manifest digest, and it has a
short lifetime. The fixed key below is tutorial material; production hosts
load a rotated key from their own secret system.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.{Attestation, ReplayStore, ToolGateway}

ReplayStore.clear()
now = ~U[2026-08-19 10:00:00.000Z]
confirmation_key = :crypto.hash(:sha256, "livebook-confirmation-demo-only")

{:ok, token} =
  ToolGateway.issue_confirmation(
    confirmation_request,
    context,
    pending,
    confirmation_key,
    now: now,
    manifest: pending.audit_metadata.manifest_digest,
    nonce: String.duplicate("c", 32),
    ttl_ms: 60_000
  )

approved_request = Attestation.attach_confirmation(confirmation_request, token)

approved =
  ToolGateway.guard_request(approved_request, context,
    confirmation_opts ++ [confirmation_key: confirmation_key, now: now]
  )

:allowed = approved.verdict
:accepted = approved.audit_metadata.confirmation_status

%{verdict: approved.verdict, confirmation: approved.audit_metadata.confirmation_status}
```

**On stage:** reuse the same request immediately. A token is one-shot by
default; the second call is a replay, not another authorization.

```elixir
replay =
  ToolGateway.guard_request(approved_request, context,
    confirmation_opts ++ [confirmation_key: confirmation_key, now: now]
  )

:blocked = replay.verdict
:replay_detected = replay.audit_metadata.confirmation_reason

%{verdict: replay.verdict, reason: replay.audit_metadata.confirmation_reason}
```

Approval also dies if the payload changes. We disable consumption here only so
the cell isolates binding from replay protection; production should keep the
one-shot default.

```elixir
changed_after_approval =
  approved_request
  |> put_in(["params", "arguments", "path"], "lib/changed_after_approval.ex")

changed =
  ToolGateway.guard_request(changed_after_approval, context,
    confirmation_opts ++
      [confirmation_key: confirmation_key, consume_confirmation: false, now: now]
  )

:blocked = changed.verdict
:digest_mismatch = changed.audit_metadata.confirmation_reason

%{verdict: changed.verdict, reason: changed.audit_metadata.confirmation_reason}
```

## Attest What Was Allowed

Confirmation answers “may this run?” Attestation answers “what did this host
observe and decide?” This deterministic signer keeps the tutorial repeatable.
Real deployments should use a protected Ed25519 key and publish its trust
material through their normal identity system.

```elixir
defmodule GatewayTalkSigner do
  @behaviour SigilGuard.Signer
  @seed :crypto.hash(:sha256, "sigil-guard-livebook-gateway-signer")

  @impl true
  def sign(message) do
    {_public, private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, @seed)
    :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, message, [private, :ed25519])
  end

  @impl true
  def public_key do
    {public, _private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, @seed)
    public
  end
end

{:ok, envelope} =
  ToolGateway.attest_request(allowed, context,
    payload: request,
    signer: GatewayTalkSigner,
    keyid: "conference-demo",
    now: now,
    nonce: "conference-request-attestation",
    manifest: capability,
    resource: "mcp://repo-mcp",
    audience: "repo-mcp",
    scopes: ["repo:write"]
  )

trust_material = %{"conference-demo" => GatewayTalkSigner.public_key()}

{:ok, statement} =
  Attestation.verify(envelope, trust_material,
    payload: SigilGuard.MCP.SecurityPayload.for_gate(request, :request),
    context: context,
    manifest_digest: capability.digest,
    now: now
  )

%{
  envelope_type: envelope["payloadType"],
  statement_type: statement["predicate"]["statement_type"],
  subject_digest: get_in(statement, ["subject", Access.at(0), "digest", "sha256"]),
  manifest_digest: statement["predicate"]["tool"]["manifest_digest"]
}
```

An inbound peer can require that envelope before accepting the request. The
transport field `_agent_trust` is deliberately separate from user arguments.

```elixir
attested_request = Attestation.attach(request, envelope)

accepted_attestation =
  ToolGateway.guard_request(attested_request, context,
    manifests: %{capability.name => capability},
    require_manifest: true,
    attestation: :required,
    trust_material: trust_material,
    now: now
  )

:allowed = accepted_attestation.verdict

missing_attestation =
  ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context,
    manifests: %{capability.name => capability},
    require_manifest: true,
    attestation: :required,
    trust_material: trust_material,
    now: now
  )

:blocked = missing_attestation.verdict
:invalid_attestation = missing_attestation.audit_metadata.deny_reason

%{
  signed: accepted_attestation.verdict,
  unsigned: missing_attestation.verdict,
  unsigned_reason: missing_attestation.audit_metadata.deny_reason
}
```

## What To Take Away

The manifest binds the tool definition. The confirmation binds temporary human
authority. The attestation binds portable evidence. None of them transfers
callback ownership to the model, and none replaces the runtime boundary gate.

```elixir
%{
  pinned_manifest: capability.digest,
  drift: drifted.verdict,
  explicit_approval: approved.verdict,
  replay: replay.verdict,
  signed_evidence: accepted_attestation.verdict
}
```
